#!/usr/bin/env sh
# The full app unit suite, once per push, and nothing else.
#
# It is here rather than in `gate:commit` because the commit gate runs no app
# test at all, so it cannot approximate this: a commit can pass the commit gate
# and still land a change whose own test was already failing before the commit
# started. `npm run test` is also the on-demand full scan — this hook is a
# reminder that it exists, not a second place to configure it.
#
# `tsc` and `build` are deliberately NOT repeated here. An earlier version
# re-ran all of `gate:commit` and bought nothing with it: a rebase or a
# `--no-verify` commit that broke the build is caught by the `ci` job, and
# re-running several seconds of deterministic checks to protect a shorter suite
# run is how a gate ends up `--no-verify`'d along with everything else.
#
# WHAT THIS HOOK IS NOT ALLOWED TO ASK, which is the one rule here that has been
# broken. It must not run a test whose answer depends on the state of the
# operator's machine. It did: the worktree-inventory suite read git in every
# worktree on the machine, so this gate went red because the main checkout had
# one modified file at that instant, needed a 60s budget per test to survive its
# own git spawns, and on a `pull_request` CI checkout — where no `main` ref
# exists — took three tests down with an unguarded `rev-parse`. The consequence
# was a real `--no-verify` push of 285 commits to `main` with this gate
# unverified, which is worse than having no gate here. The suite now asserts the
# same rules over a synthetic fleet it builds, and the question about the live
# fleet is `npm run inventory:verify`, which is a report.
#
# `scripts/__tests__/gates-wiring.test.mjs` asserts that the hook runs a script
# that exists, that it does not swallow a failure, and that the suite it runs has
# no per-test budget and no read of the live worktree list — so the three failure
# modes above cannot come back quietly.
set -e

npm run gate:push
